course: books
lesson: 14
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter VII, part 2: A Mad Tea-Party (the treacle-well and the garden at last)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Dormouse starts singing «Twinkle, twinkle» in his sleep and won't stop till they pinch him. The Hatter finishes his tale: the Queen screamed «He's murdering the time! Off with his head!», and ever since, Time won't help him. It is always six o'clock, always tea-time, so they never wash up; they just move round the table to clean cups.
explain: Alice asks what happens when they get back to the start, and the March Hare quickly changes the subject. They wake the Dormouse for a story: three sisters, Elsie, Lacie and Tillie, who live at the bottom of a well and eat nothing but treacle. Alice keeps asking sensible questions, and the answers get sillier and sillier.
explain: When Alice says there is no such thing as a treacle-well, the Dormouse sulks; she promises not to interrupt, and breaks the promise almost at once. The Hatter wants a clean cup, everyone moves one place on, and Alice ends up at the March Hare's messy place, where the milk-jug has been knocked over.
explain: The jokes are word tricks: the sisters «draw» treacle from the well, and also «draw» pictures of things that begin with M, like the moon, memory and «muchness». When Alice starts «I don't think—», the Hatter snaps «Then you shouldn't talk», and that is too rude for her. She walks off; looking back, she sees them trying to stuff the Dormouse into the teapot.
explain: «The stupidest tea-party I ever was at», she says. Then she finds a door in a tree, comes back to the long hall from chapter one, and this time does it in the right order: key first, then mushroom, down to a foot high, through the little passage, and at last into the beautiful garden with bright flower-beds and cool fountains.
fact: When the Hatter sang at the concert, the Queen «bawled out» (shouted) «He's murdering the time! Off with his head!»; to murder the time also means to sing it badly out of rhythm.
fact: «It's always six o'clock now,» says the Hatter; Alice has a bright idea: «Is that the reason so many tea-things are put out here?», and the Hatter says yes; so it is always tea-time, and there is no time to wash the things «between whiles» (in between).
fact: They move round the table to clean places as the things get used up; when Alice asks what happens when they come back to the beginning, the March Hare says «Suppose we change the subject».
fact: The Dormouse's three sisters are Elsie, Lacie and Tillie; they live at the bottom of a well, on treacle; Alice asks what they lived on because she «always took a great interest in questions of eating and drinking»; treacle is thick sweet syrup, like golden syrup or molasses.
fact: Alice says they would have been ill; the Dormouse answers «So they were, very ill».
fact: «Take some more tea,» says the March Hare; Alice has had none, so she «can't take more»; the Hatter says she means she can't take less, because «it's very easy to take more than nothing».
fact: Alice snaps «Nobody asked your opinion», and the Hatter asks «Who's making personal remarks now?» — she has done the very thing she scolded him for.
fact: When Alice says a treacle-well is no such thing, the Dormouse sulks: «If you can't be civil, you'd better finish the story for yourself»; Alice says «I dare say there may be one» (I dare say = I suppose).
fact: «Draw» has two meanings: to pull liquid up out of a well, and to make pictures; the Dormouse uses both at once; Alice asks «What did they draw?», «quite forgetting her promise» not to interrupt.
fact: «Well in» is a pun: the sisters are in the well, and «well in» also means deep inside.
fact: When the Hatter wants a clean cup, all move one place on; only the Hatter gains, and Alice is worse off, because the March Hare had upset the milk-jug into his plate.
fact: The sisters draw everything that begins with M: «mouse-traps, and the moon, and memory, and muchness»; «much of a muchness» is an old saying for nearly the same.
fact: The Hatter's «Then you shouldn't talk» is «more than Alice could bear»; she leaves «in great disgust» before the story ends, the Dormouse falls asleep at once, and the others are trying to put the Dormouse into the teapot.
fact: She finds a door in a tree, comes back to the long hall, takes the little golden key, unlocks the door, nibbles mushroom till she is «about a foot high», and walks through into the garden.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bruv» (London) = mate, brother; «sus» = suspicious, odd; «wee» (Scotland) = small; «bairn» (Scotland, North East) = child; «delulu» = in a dream world, not facing facts.
exercise: What does the Dormouse do when the Hatter mentions «Twinkle, twinkle»? | Sings «Twinkle, twinkle» in his sleep until they pinch him | Wakes up at once and asks the March Hare for a big spoon of treacle | Runs off to tell the Queen | Hides under the table-cloth
exercise: What did the Queen shout when the Hatter sang? | «He's murdering the time! Off with his head!» | «Sing it again, louder this time, and in tune, please!» | «Who stole the tarts?» | «Twinkle, twinkle, little star!»
exercise: What time is it always now at the tea-party? | Six o'clock | Half-past one, dinner-time | Nine o'clock, time for lessons | Midnight, when the Dormouse sleeps
exercise: Alice has «a bright idea». What does she guess? | That is why so many tea-things are put out on the table | That the Hatter is really the White Rabbit in a tall hat | That the Dormouse is the one who quarrelled with Time | That the Queen of Hearts lives in the March Hare's house
exercise: How do they deal with dirty cups, since there is no time to wash up? | They keep moving round the table to clean places | They throw them at the Dormouse | They lick them clean like cats | They take them down to the treacle-well and wash them there
exercise: Alice asks what happens «when you come to the beginning again». What happens? | The March Hare changes the subject | The Hatter gives a long answer about Time | The Dormouse explains it in his sleep | Alice works it out for herself
exercise: What are the names of the three little sisters in the Dormouse's story? | Elsie, Lacie and Tillie | Ada, Mabel and Alice | Mary Ann, Dinah and Pat | Five, Seven and Two
exercise: Where do the sisters live? | At the bottom of a well | At the top of a tall tree | Inside a big teapot | In the Queen's garden
exercise: What do the sisters live on? | Treacle | Bread-and-butter and tea, like the Hatter | Mushrooms | Pepper soup
exercise: Why does Alice ask about food in the story? | She always took a great interest in questions of eating and drinking | She was very hungry, as the March Hare had eaten all the bread-and-butter on the table before she came | She wanted to know if Dinah would like it | She thought treacle was a kind of fish
exercise: «They'd have been ill,» says Alice. What does the Dormouse answer? | «So they were, very ill» | «No, treacle is good for you» | «They were never ill in their lives» | «Ask the Hatter, he knows»
exercise: Alice has had no tea yet. The March Hare says «Take some more tea». What is the muddle? | You can't take «more» when you've had none, though the Hatter says it's easy to take more than nothing | She has had five cups already and can't drink another | There is no tea left in the pot | The tea is really wine
exercise: «Nobody asked your opinion,» says Alice. How does the Hatter turn it on her? | «Who's making personal remarks now?» | «Then you shouldn't talk» | «Your hair wants cutting» | «Off with her head!»
exercise: What does Alice say about a treacle-well, which upsets the Dormouse? | «There's no such thing!» | «I'd like to visit one» | «It must be very sticky» | «My sister has one at home»
exercise: «I dare say there may be one,» says Alice. What does «I dare say» mean here? | I suppose | I am brave enough | I refuse | I promise
exercise: Why does the Hatter want everyone to move one place on? | He wants a clean cup | He wants to sit by Alice | The sun is in his eyes | The Dormouse snores too loud
exercise: Who is worse off after everyone moves one place on? | Alice, because the March Hare had just upset the milk-jug into his plate | The Hatter, because now his cup is dirtier than before | The Dormouse, because he lands right next to the hot teapot and the spilt sugar-bowl | The March Hare, because he has to sit on the Dormouse
exercise: The sisters «draw» treacle from the well. What is the joke in «draw»? | It means pulling liquid out of a well, and also making pictures | It means a game where nobody wins, like a draw at football | It means a drawer in the raven's writing-desk where the sisters kept their treacle | It means pulling a cart full of treacle along the road
exercise: «But they were in the well,» says Alice. «Of course they were, — well in.» What is the pun? | «Well in» means deep inside, and they are in a well | «Well» means healthy, so they were not ill after all | «Well» is a kind of treacle in old English | «Well in» means it is time to go home
exercise: Which of these did the sisters draw? | The moon | A raven | A writing-desk | A flamingo
exercise: Why does Alice finally get up and leave? | The Hatter says «Then you shouldn't talk», which is more than she can bear | The March Hare spills the milk-jug over her new dress and laughs | The Dormouse falls asleep and nobody will finish the story | She hears the Queen calling her to play croquet
exercise: What are the others doing the last time Alice looks back? | Trying to put the Dormouse into the teapot | Calling after her to come back and finish her tea | Washing up all the cups | Singing «Twinkle, twinkle»
exercise: What does Alice find in the wood after the tea-party? | A tree with a door leading right into it | A little bottle with a paper label marked DRINK ME | The White Rabbit's house | A large blue caterpillar
exercise: Where does the door in the tree lead? | Back to the long hall with the little glass table | Straight into the middle of the Queen's croquet-ground | Down into the treacle-well | Into the Duchess's kitchen
exercise: In what order does Alice do things in the hall this time? | Takes the key, unlocks the door, then nibbles the mushroom to a foot high | Nibbles the mushroom first, then climbs up the table leg to the key | Drinks from a bottle, then cries a pool of tears | Eats a cake, grows nine feet tall, then breaks the door
exercise: What is the garden like when she reaches it? | Bright flower-beds and cool fountains | Dark and low, with locked doors all round it | Full of pepper and smoke | A sandy beach with lobsters
test: Sam: «the Hatter's song, bruv, the King said off with his head.» (bruv = mate) Put it right. | It was the Queen who bawled «Off with his head!» | The King said it, and then the Queen said sorry to the Hatter | The March Hare said it as a joke | Time said it, out of the clock
test: «Murdering the time» — what is the joke? | Singing out of rhythm is called murdering it, but Time sounds like a person who could be hurt | The Hatter really did hurt a man called Time with his hat, and he was sent to prison for it at once | The Queen meant the Hatter had broken her clock with a hammer | It means the Hatter came very late to the concert
test: What would happen, by the tea-party's rules, if they used up all the clean places? | The book doesn't say: the March Hare changes the subject when Alice asks | They would wash every cup in the treacle-well and then start again from the Hatter's first seat | The Queen would send new cups every hour on a cushion | Time would forgive the Hatter and make it seven o'clock
test: Jess: «Alice was dead polite the whole time, never said owt rude.» (owt = anything, North England) Is that right? | No: she snapped «Nobody asked your opinion», a personal remark of her own | Yes: she never spoke a single rude word at the table, not even when the Hatter was rude to her | Yes: she only spoke to the Dormouse, never to the others | No: she threw her cup at the Hatter in a temper
test: «They lived on treacle» — «They'd have been ill» — «So they were». What makes this funny? | The Dormouse agrees with Alice's objection instead of changing the story | The Dormouse is talking about real children he knows, who live just down the road from the March Hare | Alice is actually asking for some treacle | The sisters are only ill on Sundays
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «Alice keeps her promise not to interrupt the Dormouse again.» | False: she asks «What did they draw?» at once, «quite forgetting her promise» | True: she sits quietly right to the end of the story, then thanks the Dormouse and claps politely | True: the Hatter interrupts instead of her, every single time | False: she leaves the table before she has promised anything
test: Ellie from Glasgow: «the wee sisters drew stuff beginning wi' T, like treacle.» (wee = little) Put it right. | They drew things beginning with M, like mouse-traps, the moon, memory and muchness | They drew things beginning with T, like treacle, tea, tea-trays, teapots and the Hatter's tall top hat | They drew only pictures of the well and the treacle | They drew nothing, because it was too dark down the well
test: Why can't you draw «a muchness»? | «Much of a muchness» is just a saying for nearly the same, not a thing you can see | A muchness is far too big to fit on any page in a sketch-book, even a giant one | Muchness is a secret drawing only the Queen may see | You can, but only in treacle at the bottom of a well
test: «Really, now you ask me, I don't think—» «Then you shouldn't talk.» Why is that so rude? | He twists her «I don't think» to mean she has no thoughts, so she should shut up | He is telling her she has talked quite enough about wells and treacle for one day and should eat | He is asking her politely to let the Dormouse speak instead | He thinks she is speaking French and can't understand her
test: Callum, kinda sus (suspicious): «Alice stayed for the end of the story, right?» Put it right. | No, she walked off in disgust before it ended, and the Dormouse fell asleep | Yes, she stayed until the Hatter said goodbye and bowed | Yes, the story ended with the sisters coming out of the well | No, the Queen came by with her soldiers and took everyone off to the croquet-ground
test: Alice was rude at the start of the tea-party and rude again later. Who started the rudeness? | The March Hare and the Hatter, with «No room!» and «Your hair wants cutting» | Alice, who sat down and asked for treacle at once | The Dormouse, who snored very loudly at her | Nobody: they were all very polite to each other until the Queen of Hearts came along
test: Looking back: in chapter III Alice also spoiled someone's story by interrupting. Whose? | The Mouse's «long and sad tale», when she mixed up «not» and «knot» | The Caterpillar's tale of Father William, when she yawned and looked away | The Duchess's lullaby, when she sneezed at the pepper | The Pigeon's tale, when she called it a serpent
test: Looking back: what did Alice do wrong the first time she was small enough for the little door, in chapter I? | She forgot the golden key on the glass table, and couldn't reach it | She forgot the mushroom on top of the Caterpillar's mushroom | She shut the door and lost the key down the rabbit-hole | She drank too much and grew nine feet tall
test: Looking back: where did Alice get the piece of mushroom she nibbles in the hall? | From the Caterpillar's mushroom in the wood: one side makes you taller, the other shorter | From the Duchess's kitchen, where the cook was making mushroom soup with far too much pepper | From the March Hare's table, next to the bread-and-butter | From the glass box under the table, marked EAT ME
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, Alice made a plan in the wood. What were its two parts? | First grow to her right size, then find the way into the lovely garden | First find Dinah the cat, then go home to her sister and have tea by the fire | First find the White Rabbit's gloves, then the fan | First visit the Hatter, then the March Hare
test: Looking back: who else has threatened «off with» someone's head before this chapter? | The Duchess, in chapter VI: «Talking of axes, chop off her head!» | The Mouse, in chapter III, when the Lory shivered | The Caterpillar, in chapter V, when Alice nearly lost her temper with him | Bill the Lizard, in chapter IV, when he flew up the chimney
test: Looking back: how high was the little door in chapter I? | About fifteen inches | About fifteen feet | About two miles | About four inches
test: ★★★ Before moving, the order round the corner is: Hatter, Dormouse, March Hare, Alice. Each moves into the next place on. Whose old place does Alice take? | The March Hare's | The Dormouse's own place | The Hatter's | Her own again
test: ★★★ The sisters draw things beginning with M. Which list fits the rule without a mistake? | Mouse-traps, the moon, memory | Moon, treacle, memory | Mouse-traps, muchness, teapots | Memory, Mabel, milk-jug, Dinah
test: ★★★ «It's very easy to take more than nothing.» If Alice had nothing and then took some tea, which is true? | She took more than nothing but could not take less than nothing | She took less than nothing, which is impossible, so she took no tea at all | She took nothing and more at the same time | She took more tea than the Hatter ever had
exam: Identify the circumstance which, according to the Hatter, gave rise to the perpetual condition of six o'clock. | The Queen's pronouncement that he was murdering the time, after which Time declined to oblige him | The loss of his watch in the March Hare's tea, which occasioned a delay of two days in its working | The Dormouse's continued slumber at the table | The arrival of Alice without an invitation
exam: It is stated that the three sisters «lived on treacle». Ascertain the condition in which they were consequently said to be. | Very ill | In excellent health and spirits | Asleep for the greater part of each day | Exceedingly wealthy, owing to the sale of treacle
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the disadvantage incurred by Alice when the party moved one place on. | Her new place was spoiled by milk upset into the plate by the March Hare | She was obliged to sit next to the Queen of Hearts, who was at that time a guest at the table | She received the Dormouse's dirty cup and saucer | She was required to tell a story she did not know
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning Alice's progress after quitting the tea-party? | She entered the garden by means of the golden key and the mushroom | She returned to the bank where her sister sat reading a book without pictures or conversations | She fell once more down the rabbit-hole | She went to the house of the White Rabbit
